From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 10:51:49 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 175/180] fix(gastown): refinery fences run in ONE Bash call,
 never inside bash -c (gcp-xg2mh)

Claude Code's rm safety check denies a refinery fence about 24x as often when
the session wraps it in bash -c '<script>' (5 of 14 wrapped variable-target
calls denied, against 6 of 402 run directly). Each denial costs a 2-minute
prompt timeout, and the session then re-runs the step its own way, so the
formula's fence does not run as written. Found on gcp-4fcl.

The formula invites the wrap: seven run sites in mol-refinery-patrol.toml say
to run a fence "in ONE shell" / "in one shell". The intent is one Bash tool
call, so shell variables carry across the block, but a session can read it as
"wrap the block in bash -c". Each site now says "in ONE Bash call, never
inside `bash -c`". No other character changes and nothing is re-wrapped.

The two sentences test_refinery_acceptance_wiring.sh pins byte for byte are
updated the same way. A new case, fences_name_one_bash_call, fails if any run
site says "in one shell" again, needs at least seven sites naming the new
phrase, and proves it can fail by putting the first site back to "in ONE
shell" in a copy. EXPECTED_CASES goes 16 -> 17.

Out of scope, per the bead: mol-polecat-work.toml's own run site (no polecat
denial evidence), and guarding : > "$GATE_LOG" (32 of 32 direct runs passed).
Fork code, so patches/ is re-exported and folded into this commit.

Refs: gcp-4fcl

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
